[38;2;124;124;124mX| = \prod_{k=1}^n (k+1) = 2 \cdot 3 \cdots (n+1) = (n+1)!/1 = (n+1)!$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait: $\prod_{k=1}^n (k+1) = 2 \cdot 3 \cdot 4 \cdots (n+1) = (n+1)!/1! = (n+1)![0m[38;2;124;124;124m$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $|X| = (n+1)!$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe need a proper subset $A \subsetneq X$ such that $A$ is closed under $\vee$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mand $\wedge$ (i.e., $A$ is a sublattice). We want the largest such proper [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msublattice.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA sublattice of a finite lattice that is closed under both join and meet. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mlargest proper sublattice.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this. A sublattice must contain the join and meet of any two [0m[38;2;124;124;124mof its elements. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey insight: If $A$ is a sublattice, it need not contain $\hat{0} = (0,0,[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\ldots,0)$ or $\hat{1} = (1,2,3,\ldots,n)$ (the top element). Wait, actually a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msublattice doesn't need to contain the top or bottom of the original lattice.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut if $A$ contains two elements, it contains their join and meet. If $A$ is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnonempty, consider the join of all elements of $A$ — that's in $A$ only if we [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcan build it up by pairwise joins, which we can since $A$ is finite and closed [0m
[38;2;124;124;124munder joins. So the join of all elements of $A$ is in $A$, call it $u$. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSimilarly the meet of all elements of $A$ is in $A$, call it $l$. So $A$ has a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtop $u$ and bottom $l$ (as a lattice in its own right).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, to maximize $|A|$, we want to remove as few elements as possible from $X$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhile keeping it a sublattice.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mOne approach: Remove a single element. Can we remove one element and still have [0m[38;2;124;124;124ma sublattice? We need: for any $s, t \in A = X \setminus \{x\}$, $s \vee t \neq [0m[38;2;124;124;124mx$ and $s \wedge t \neq x$. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $x$ should not be expressible as $s \vee t$ or $s \wedge t$ for $s, t \neq [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mx$. This means $x$ is join-irreducible and meet-irreducible in $X$ (i.e., $x$ is[0m[38;2;124;124;124mboth join-irreducible and meet-irreducible).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn a product of chains, an element is join-irreducible if it has exactly one [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcomponent that is not the minimum of its chain... wait, let me think more [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcarefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, in a finite lattice, $x$ is join-irreducible if $x \neq \hat{0}$ and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$x = a \vee b$ implies $x = a$ or $x = b$. In a product of chains, the join-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mirreducibles are elements that are nonzero in exactly one component.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHmm wait, that's for the case where the lattice is a product of chains and we [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconsider the standard join-irreducibles. Let me reconsider.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn a chain $C_k = \{0, 1, \ldots, k\}$, the join-irreducibles are $\{1, 2, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\ldots, k\}$ (every nonzero element, since in a chain $a \vee b = \max(a,b)$, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mand $\max(a,b) = x$ with $a, b < x$ is impossible unless one equals $x$). [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mActually in a chain, $x$ is join-irreducible iff $x \neq 0$ (the bottom). [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mBecause if $x = a \vee b = \max(a,b)$, then $\max(a,b) = x$ means $a = x$ or $b [0m[38;2;124;124;124m= x$. So yes, every non-bottom element is join-irreducible.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn a product of chains $L = C_1 \times C_2 \times \cdots \times C_n$, the join-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mirreducibles are elements that are nonzero in exactly one component. Because if [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$x = (x_1, \ldots, x_n)$ is nonzero in two or more components, say $x_i > 0$ and[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$x_j > 0$, then $x = a \vee b$ where $a$ has $x_i$ in position $i$ and $0$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124melsewhere, $b$ has $x_j$ in position $j$ and $0$ elsewhere (and other components[0m[38;2;124;124;124mas needed). Actually more precisely, $x$ is join-irreducible in a product iff [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mit's nonzero in exactly one component.[0m

